// The same rule as tests/xfail/generic_unbounded_call, and the case it exists for: the generic is CALLED, with a
// type that has `get`. An instance cannot vouch for its template — `T` promises nothing, so `w.get()` is an error at
// the declaration whichever types the program happens to pass. Until 0.9.571 the walk skipped every instantiated
// template, so this built: a library generic passed `kama check` exactly when its own tests instantiated it, and
// failed in its own body for the first consumer type without `get`.
type value W {
    int32 n = 7;
    public ctor make() { }
    public fn int32 get() { return this.n; }
}
fn int32 feed<T>(T w) { return w.get(); }   // ERROR: `T` is unbounded, so it promises nothing
fn int32 main() { return feed(w: W.make()); }
